"Distinguishes growth directions by whether products and markets are existing or new relative to a defined business.\n\nUse when: Compare broad growth options before detailed investment analysis. Clarify whether a proposal changes the offer, market or both.\n\nAvoid when: The team needs numerical risk estimates or a complete competitive strategy.\n\nVisual grammar: Two axes distinguish existing and new products and markets. Four cells identify penetration, market development, product development and diversification. Proposals are placed with explicit baseline definitions.\n\nBuild: Define what existing product and existing market mean for this business. Place concrete growth proposals in the appropriate cells. Discuss required capabilities, assumptions and evidence for each direction.\n\nCheck: Newness is assessed relative to the stated business baseline. Market development and product development remain distinct. Cell placement is not presented as a quantified risk calculation.\n\nPitfalls: Classifying a minor variant as an entirely new product without criteria. Assuming diversification is one uniform opportunity class.\n\nExample: A bakery compares selling more existing bread locally, supplying hotels, adding new pastries and entering an unrelated food service.\n\nAlternatives: Business Model Canvas, GE–McKinsey matrix."
